# Copy to `.env` for local use. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so it
# must never contain a real value — only names and explanations.
#
# Which of these belong where:
#
#   Netlify  → Site configuration → Environment variables
#   CI       → repository secrets
#   Local    → your shell

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing. Every dependency resolves from the public npm registry or from this
# workspace, so builds need no credentials at all — not locally, not in CI,
# not on Netlify.

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which ContentAdapter / AuthProvider the shell injects at server start.
# Both default to the backend-free option, so a fresh clone runs with no
# setup at all. Set either to "supabase" once you have a local (`supabase
# start`) or hosted project — see supabase/README.md.
# CONTENT_ADAPTER=memory
# AUTH_PROVIDER=mock

# Required when CONTENT_ADAPTER or AUTH_PROVIDER is "supabase". Note the split
# carefully:
#
#   SUPABASE_URL              safe to expose; identifies the project
#   SUPABASE_ANON_KEY         safe to expose; RLS is what protects the data
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY SERVER ONLY — bypasses RLS entirely
#
# Vite inlines any variable prefixed `VITE_` into the client bundle, where it
# is readable by anyone. The service role key must never carry that prefix,
# and must never be read outside a server function. Nothing in apps/shell
# currently reads it at all — both Supabase adapters use only the anon key,
# since every table they touch is either public-read RLS (content) or
# protected by the caller's own session (auth). It is listed here for the
# contract suites (`content-adapter-supabase`, `auth-supabase`), which use it
# to seed and tear down fixtures with `supabase.auth.admin` — see each
# package's `src/contract.test.ts`.
# SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
